# Building a rule

Open **Alerts** in the sidebar and press **New alert**. Four steps make the
alert:

1. **Type.** Choose what the alert is for, such as Low, High or No readings.
   **Build your own**, under **More alert types**, opens the full set of
   conditions.
2. **Settings.** Set the level, how long to wait before the alert starts, and
   the hours it applies to.
3. **Delivery.** Choose how urgent the alert is and where it goes.
4. **Check.** Nocturne shows how many times the alert would have started in
   your last 14 days, with a chart of the most recent time. Send a test alert
   to make sure that it arrives, then press **Create alert**. The name is
   written for you. You can change it.

To change a rule, or to set auto-resolve and smart snooze, pick the rule in the
list. The editor is one page with a card for each part of the rule. Nothing
takes effect until you press **Save**.

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-editor" />

## Name and severity

<Screenshot
  id="alert-rule-identity"
  callouts={[
    { anchor: 'enabled', label: 'Enabled' },
    { anchor: 'severity', label: 'Severity' },
    { anchor: 'allow-dnd', label: 'Also alert during Do Not Disturb' },
  ]}
/>

- **Name** is what the alert says when it reaches you, so make it something
  you will understand half asleep: "Low overnight", not "Rule 3".
- **Description** is optional. Use it to note why the rule exists.
- **Enabled** turns the rule on or off. A rule that is off is never checked,
  and switching a rule off closes any alert it has open.
- **Severity** says how urgent the alert is. It changes what happens when the
  alert fires:

| Severity | What it does |
|----------|--------------|
| **Critical** | Always gets through Do not disturb. Use it for the alerts that must never be silenced, such as an urgent low. |
| **Warning** | The default. Silenced by Do not disturb unless you tick the box below. |
| **Info** | Sent once and then marked as acknowledged straight away. It will not keep nagging. Use it for reminders, such as an old pump site. |

- **Also alert during Do Not Disturb** lets a Warning or Info rule reach you even
  while Do not disturb is on. Critical rules always get through, so the box
  makes no difference to them.

## Condition

The condition is what the rule watches for. It reads as a sentence: "Notify
when **all of** / **any of** these are true", followed by one row per
condition.

<Screenshot
  id="alert-rule-sustained-low"
  callouts={[
    { anchor: 'operator', label: 'all of / any of' },
    { anchor: 'sustained', label: 'for at least' },
  ]}
/>

Each row starts with a word saying how it joins the rows above it: **IF** for
the first row, then **AND** or **OR** depending on the choice at the top. Then
comes the thing being checked, with its settings.

Press the add button under the rows to add a condition. Conditions are grouped
by what they watch: glucose, insulin, carbs, your devices, what your pump or
loop is doing, time, and other alerts.

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-add-picker" />

Every row has a menu (the **...** button at its end) with the ways you can
change it:

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-row-actions" />

- **Put in an “all of” group** and **Put in an “any of” group** put the row inside a group of
  its own, so you can build rules like "A, or both B and C". See
  [Combining conditions](/docs/alerts/combining).
- **Make it the opposite (NOT)** turns the row into its opposite.
- **Add “for at least”** adds a "for at least N min" box: the condition then has
  to stay true for that long before it counts. See
  [Conditions that must last](/docs/alerts/sustained).
- **Remove** deletes the row. Holding Shift while hovering a row also shows a
  delete button.

You can drag rows by the handle at their left to reorder them, or drop one onto
a group to move it inside. The order of rows does not change what a rule does.

Every condition, with what it means and when to use it, is on the
[Conditions](/docs/alerts/conditions) page.

<Callout type="info" title="Values are shown in your units">

  Glucose values in the editor follow the unit you have chosen in your settings,
  mg/dL or mmol/L. Nocturne stores them in mg/dL and converts when it shows
  them, so a rule written in one unit reads correctly in the other.

</Callout>

## Channels

Channels are where the alert goes. A rule can have several, and they are all
sent at the same moment. A rule with no channels still fires and is still
recorded in alert history; it just does not notify anyone.

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-channels" />

| Channel | Where the alert goes |
|---------|----------------------|
| **In-app** | The Nocturne notification center of the member chosen as the channel's recipient. A new channel goes to you unless you pick someone else on the site. |
| **Discord**, **Slack**, **Telegram**, **WhatsApp** | A direct message to a linked account, or a post in a channel or group the Nocturne bot has joined. See [Chat bots](/docs/bots). |
| **Email** | An email to an address you choose. Needs email set up first; see [Email alerts](/docs/alerts/email). |
| **Webhook** | A message sent to a web address you control, for connecting other systems. |
| **Device** | An instruction sent to a device you have registered with Nocturne. |

The channels on offer depend on what your site has set up. A chat channel only
appears once that chat app is connected.

### When a rule cannot run

If Nocturne cannot evaluate the condition of an enabled rule, the rule cannot
fire until you edit it. Nocturne then sends one notice through the rule's
channels to tell its recipients. It sends the notice again only when the
condition changes, or when you disable the rule and enable it again. A
**Device** channel does not get the notice. The notice clears when you fix the
condition, disable the rule or delete it.

### Webhook messages

A webhook channel sends each message as a JSON object. The `Kind` field tells
you what the message is:

| `Kind` | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `excursion` | The rule's condition became true. `GlucoseValue`, `Trend` and `TrendRate` hold the reading. |
| `unevaluable` | The rule cannot run until it is edited. `GlucoseValue`, `Trend` and `TrendRate` are null, and `ReadingTimestamp` is the time of the notice. |
| `held` | The rule has waited 24 hours for missing data. As for `unevaluable`, and `HeldFacts` lists each missing data source as `Fact` and `Reason`. |
| `held_closed` | As `held`, for a rule with an open alert, which Nocturne closes because its data has been missing for 24 hours. |

The other fields are the same for both kinds: `RuleName`, `Severity`,
`AlertType`, `ExcursionId`, `InstanceId`, `TenantId`, `SubjectName` and
`ActiveExcursionCount`. A receiver that does not know a value of `Kind` must
not show the message as a glucose alert.

## Auto-resolve

Auto-resolve closes an alert when a second condition becomes true, even if the
rule's own condition still holds. Most rules do not need it: an alert already
closes by itself as soon as its condition stops being true.

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-auto-resolve" />

Auto-resolve is for "stop telling me once this has happened". For example: a
high alert that should stop once glucose has been coming down steadily for 15
minutes, even though it is still above the line. How it behaves, including what
happens if things get worse again, is on
[When an alert fires](/docs/alerts/when-an-alert-fires#auto-resolve).

**Suggest** fills in a starting condition by turning your rule's condition
around. Treat it as a draft. The exact opposite of your condition closes the
alert at the same moment it would have closed anyway.

## Smart snooze

Snoozing silences an alert for a while. Smart snooze keeps extending that
snooze, a few minutes at a time, while conditions you choose hold. The usual use
is "I have treated this low; stay quiet while glucose is climbing back".

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-smart-snooze" />

Details, including the limit on how many times a snooze can be extended, are on
[When an alert fires](/docs/alerts/when-an-alert-fires#snoozing).

## Advanced

The **Advanced** section is closed until you open it. It holds two settings
for what a rule does when its data is missing. When a rule has a setting that is
not the default, the closed section shows it.

### When data is missing

These choices apply only when missing data stops the rule from knowing whether
its full condition is true. If the other parts of the condition give a result,
the rule uses that result.

- **Wait** (the default). The rule does not change the alert. It opens no alert,
  and it does not close an open alert, until it knows again. A true auto-resolve
  condition can still close the alert. The waiting has a limit of 24 hours (see
  [While a rule waits](#while-a-rule-waits)).
- **Count the condition as true.** The rule counts its full condition as true. It
  can open an alert because data is missing. A "for N minutes" part does not delay
  this alert.
- **Count the condition as false.** The rule counts its full condition as false.
  An open alert closes as it does when the condition stops being true. A condition
  that uses "not" also counts as false when data is missing.

<Callout type="warning" title="A slow data source needs its own bound">

  If your data source sends readings less often than every 5 minutes, set a bound
  for this rule. Make it at least the time between two readings plus the time a
  reading can be late. If you do not: with **Count the condition as true**, the
  rule can open an alert between two readings that arrive on time. With **Count
  the condition as false**, it can close an open alert between them.

</Callout>

### While a rule waits

When a rule with **Wait** cannot know its condition, the rules list and the rule
page show **Waiting** and the time the wait started. They also show each missing
data source and the cause. The cause is one of these: missing, too old, has a time
in the future, has a value that Nocturne does not know, or not exact (for example
a pump that reports "50+" units). An open alert that waits shows the same in the
alert banner.

The waiting has a limit of 24 hours, and you cannot change it:

- When a rule has waited for 24 hours, Nocturne sends one message through the
  rule's channels. The message gives the name of the rule and the missing data. It
  has no glucose value and nothing to acknowledge. The rule continues to wait and
  shows **Waiting** until the data comes back. If the data stops again later,
  Nocturne sends a new message after 24 more hours.
- When an open alert has waited for 24 hours, Nocturne closes the alert, and the
  message says so. The alert closes even if its condition can still be true. The
  alert history shows that it closed because its data was missing for 24 hours.
  The rule opens a new alert only when the data comes back and the condition is
  true.

A rule that you disable while it waits sends no message.

### Minutes until a glucose reading counts as missing

The rule counts a glucose reading as missing when Nocturne received it more than this
number of minutes ago. This applies to conditions on the glucose value, rate of
change, trend and glucose range. It does not apply to signal loss, data age,
predicted glucose or Smart snooze conditions.

The bound is a number of minutes. When you leave the field empty, the rule uses
the default that the setting shows. **Use the default** clears the bound you set.

This section explains what each setting does. It does not say which setting is
right for you.

### Clear margin

A clear margin changes the value at which an open alert ends. It does not change
the value at which the alert starts. You set it on one condition: open the
condition's actions menu and select **Add a clear margin**. A condition can have
a margin when it compares a value with a number. These are glucose, rate of
change, predicted glucose, insulin on board, carbs on board and reservoir. Pump
battery, uploader battery, transmitter, temp basal and sensitivity can also have
one. Conditions about time, such as signal loss or site age, cannot have one.

For example, a condition "glucose below 70 mg/dL" with a clear margin of 5 mg/dL:

- The alert starts when glucose goes below 70 mg/dL, as without a margin.
- While the alert is open, the condition stays true until glucose is 75 mg/dL or
  more. A glucose value that goes from 69 to 72 and back to 69 stays one alert.
  Without the margin, each crossing of 70 can end the alert and start a new one.
- After the alert ends, the condition is true again only below 70 mg/dL.

Inside "not", the margin moves the other way. It also keeps the open alert true
for longer. For example, "not glucose below 70 mg/dL" with a clear margin of 5
stays true until glucose goes below 65 mg/dL.

A "for at least" setting is different. With it, the condition must be true for
that time before the alert starts. A clear margin has no effect until the alert is
open, and it does not delay the start.

A condition cannot have both. "For at least" also stops a value that crosses the
line back and forth from starting many alerts. It measures one time for each
condition, and that time cannot be correct for two lines at once. When you add "for
at least" to a condition, the editor removes its clear margin. A rule that has a
margin inside "for at least" cannot run until you remove the margin.

The margin has no effect in auto-resolve and Smart snooze conditions, so the
editor does not show it there. A margin of 0 is the same as no margin. A margin
cannot be less than 0, and a condition that checks for an exact value cannot have
one. On a rate of change, the margin cannot be more than the rate. With a margin
equal to the rate, a "falling" alert stays open until glucose starts to rise. The rule summary shows the margin, for example "Glucose below 70 mg/dL, clear
margin 5 mg/dL".

This section explains what the clear margin does. It does not say if a margin is
right for you, or which margin to use.

## Testing a rule

The panel on the right has two ways to check a rule before you rely on it:

- **Send a test alert** sends a real test alert to every channel on the saved
  rule, marked as a test. Use it to check the alert actually reaches the phone
  or chat it should.
- **Replay a past day** runs the rule, including changes you have not
  saved yet, against your own past readings, and shows when it would have
  fired. Nothing is sent. See [Testing rules](/docs/alerts/testing).

Under it, **Past alerts** lists the times this rule has really fired.
Pick one to replay that day.

<Callout type="tip" title="Test with a real alert once">

  Replay proves the condition is right; only a real test alert proves the alert
  reaches you. After creating or changing an important rule, press **Send a test
  alert** and check it arrives where you expect, including on a locked phone.

</Callout>
